C = [
("h1", "15. The Passages Side by Side"),
("p", "Claim: the passages answer different problems, and none of the problems is the same. The chapter sets them in one table to show that the sayings are not one creed repeated. Each saying was made for its place."),
("sub", "15.1 The problems"),
("p", "At Philippi the problem is strife and vainglory, and the saying is the example of one who humbled himself. At Colossae the problem is philosophy and the rudiments of the world, and the sayings are that he is the image of the invisible God, the Creator, and the one in whom all the fulness dwells. In the first letter to Timothy the problem is the order of a church and what it holds up, and the saying is a short confession of six lines. In Titus the problem is the grace that teaches and the hope that is awaited, and the saying names the appearing of the great God and Saviour. In Romans 9 the problem is Paul’s grief over his kinsmen and the privileges of Israel, and the saying names Christ as the last of them."),
("sub", "15.2 The table"),
("ctbl", "The passages and their problems.", [
    ("Passage", "The problem", "What is said of Christ"),
    ("Philippians 2:5–11", "Strife and vainglory in the church", "In the form of God; took the form of a servant; exalted; every tongue confesses him Lord"),
    ("Colossians 1:15–20", "Philosophy, tradition and the rudiments of the world", "Image of the invisible God; firstborn of every creature; Creator and sustainer; head of the church; the fulness dwells in him"),
    ("Colossians 2:8–10", "The same rivals; a fulness beside Christ", "All the fulness of the Godhead dwells in him bodily; head of all principality and power"),
    ("1 Timothy 3:14–16", "How to behave in the house of God", "God manifest in the flesh; received up into glory"),
    ("Titus 2:11–14", "Grace that teaches; the blessed hope", "The great God and our Saviour Jesus Christ, who gave himself for us"),
    ("Romans 9:1–5", "Paul’s grief for Israel; the privileges of Israel", "Of the fathers according to the flesh; over all, God blessed for ever"),
    ("1 Timothy 2:5, 6", "One God and one mediator", "The man Christ Jesus, who gave himself a ransom for all"),
  ], [0.22, 0.34, 0.44]),
("sub", "15.3 What the side-by-side shows"),
("p", "Three things appear. The first is that no two passages answer the same problem, so a reader cannot say that the same teaching was repeated for each church. The second is that every problem is answered by a statement about who Christ is, which suggests that Paul thought the answer to a practical or doctrinal question began there. The third is that the statements are not interchangeable. Philippians 2 does not say the Creator, and Colossians 1 does not say the servant’s form. Each saying says what its place needs."),
("p", "John Piper reads the Colossians passage in the same way, as a vaccine against errors that diminish Christ. The volume agrees that the setting of Colossians 2 explains the force of Colossians 1, and extends the point: each of the other passages is also set against a problem, and the sayings must be read in those settings."),
("sub", "15.4 The words that recur"),
("p", "A few words recur and the volume watches them. “Form” occurs in Philippians 2:6 and 2:7. “Image” occurs in Colossians 1:15 and in Romans 8:29, where the brethren are conformed to the image of the Son. “Flesh” occurs in Romans 1:3, Romans 9:5, Colossians 1:22 and 1 Timothy 3:16. “Fulness” occurs in Colossians 1:19 and 2:9. “Saviour” occurs of both God and Christ in Titus. A shared word does not make two passages one saying, and the volume does not treat it as such. It lists the words so that a reader can see what is shared and what is not."),
("sub", "15.5 What the volume takes from the chapter"),
("bul", [
    "Each saying answers a different problem, and no two answer the same.",
    "The answer to each problem begins with a statement of who Christ is.",
    "The sayings are not interchangeable: each says what its place needs.",
    "A shared word is not a shared saying, and the volume does not merge the passages.",
  ]),
("p", "The next chapter says plainly what the passages do not say."),
]
